When there's enough memory, it creates a new structure, sets its fields, increments the allocated count, and unlocks the mutex. The `destroy_pdu()` function is responsible for freeing allocated PDU structures when they are no longer needed.

The code includes an example of using the allocator within a worker thread that continuously requests PDUs for processing. Finally, I added a function to free all PDUs and reset the allocation counters (`free_all_pdus()`) to clean up resources when necessary.']

