['Here's a skeleton implementation using C++11 with the help of `std::unordered_map` for efficient key-based lookup and `std::vector` for dynamic resizing of arrays.

```cpp
#include <memory>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>

struct Record {
    int value;
};

class RecordManager {
private:
    std::unique_ptr<long[]> offsets;
    std::unique_ptr<int[]> keys;
    std::vector<Record> records;
    size_t capacity = 16; // Initial capacity for records, offsets, and keys
    size_t numRecords = 0;

    std::unordered_map<int, size_t> keyToIndex;

public:
    RecordManager() {
        reset();
    }

    ~RecordManager() {}

    void reset() {
        offsets.reset(new long[capacity]);
        keys.reset(new int[capacity]);
        records.resize(capacity);
    }

    size_t addRecord(int key, int value) {
        if (numRecords == capacity) {
            // Resize when necessary
            auto newCapacity = std::max(2 * capacity, numRecords + 16);
            RecordManager temp;
            temp.capacity = newCapacity;
            std::swap(temp.records, records);
            temp.offsets.swap(offsets);
            temp.keys.swap(keys);
            temp.keyToIndex.swap(keyToIndex);
            capacity = newCapacity;
        }

        auto index = offsets[numRecords];
        keys[numRecords] = key;
        records[numRecords].value = value;
        keyToIndex[key] = numRecords;

        offsets[numRecords + 1] = index + offsets[numRecords] + 1; // Update next offset
        ++numRecords;

        return numRecords - 1;
    }

    const Record& getRecord(int key) const {
        auto it = keyToIndex.find(key);
        if (it == keyToIndex.end())
            throw std::runtime_error("Invalid key");

        size_t index = it->second;
        return records[index];
    }
};
```

This implementation provides a basic RecordManager class that handles dynamic allocation of arrays, efficient lookup using an `std::unordered_map`, and dynamic resizing when necessary. The `addRecord()` function adds a new record to the manager based on the given key and value, while `getRecord()` retrieves a specific record by its key.

To integrate this module into an existing application that manages multiple types of records, you can create derived classes for each type of record with their own unique keys and structures. When merging records, you may want to implement comparison functions based on the key types and ensure proper handling of duplicates or conflicts.

When it comes to cache locality and memory fragmentation, the use of `std::vector` minimizes fragmentation, as elements are contiguous in memory. To further optimize cache locality, you can align the data structures for efficient cache usage by ensuring that the size of each data structure is a multiple of the cache line size (usually 64 bytes).']

[" Here is a C++ code using the unordered_map data structure from the Standard Template Library (STL) and the boost::multi_index library for efficient hash table operations. This code creates a Record class, manages records in an unordered_map and multi_index containers, and provides functions for adding, retrieving, and updating records.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>
#include <boost/multi_index/hashed_index.hpp>
#include <boost/multi_index/list_of.hpp>
#include <boost/multi_index/member.hpp>

struct Record {
    int key;
    double value;
    long offset;
};

typedef std::unordered_map<int, Record*> recordMap;
typedef boost::multi_index::multi_index_container<
        std::vector<Record>,
        boost::multi_index::hashed_unique<boost::multi_index::member<Record, int, &Record::key>>
> recordContainer;

recordMap records;
recordContainer recordIndex;
unsigned long numRecords = 0;

void addRecord(int key, double value, long offset) {
    Record* newRec = new Record{key, value, offset};
    recordIndex.insert(newRec);
    records[key] = newRec;
    ++numRecords;
}

Record* getRecord(int key) {
    auto it = records.find(key);
    if (it != records.end()) {
        return it->second;
    }
    return nullptr;
}

void updateValue(int key, double value) {
    Record* rec = getRecord(key);
    if (rec) {
        rec->value = value;
    }
}
```

